<div style="width: 100%; background-color: #1a5276; color: white; text-align: center; padding: 15px 0; margin-bottom: 20px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 2</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 2</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Reading</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 12th Grade (CEFR: B1.3)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> My Multilingual Self</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> Language Is an Important Skill</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Reading: Reading: Comprehend complex narrative texts. Reading: Analyze articles on societal trends.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Reading: Can comprehend the main events in a narrative about bilingual life (e.g., "The student said they use both languages to communicate at home and school and explained how it helps them understand different perspectives.") Can analyze an article on multilingualism trends, identifying the author's key arguments (e.g., "The article argues that being bilingual improves job prospects and helps you connect with more people, especially in Panama's diverse communities.") Can summarize an audio segment on multilingual education in Panama, highlighting challenges and solutions (e.g., "The speaker said that students in Bocas del Toro benefit from practicing English with tourists, even though access to formal language courses can be limited.")</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 30px;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px; width: 30%; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px; width: 50%; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px; width: 20%; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays a world map and asks students to identify countries known for their multilingual populations, like Panama. Students briefly share their initial thoughts on the importance of language skills in their daily lives or future careers, connecting to "My Multilingual Self." The teacher might ask, "What are some situations where knowing more than one language is a real advantage in Panama?"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models the pronunciation of key vocabulary introduced in Lesson 1, specifically focusing on the /s/ sound in "success" and /z/ in "citizens" as well as word stress in multisyllabic words like "multilingualism," "multicultural," and "opportunities." The teacher writes the words on the board and underlines stressed syllables, asking students to repeat chorally and individually.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding of previous lesson's vocabulary, particularly related to the upcoming reading text.</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If someone has 'high proficiency' in a language, do they speak it well or just a little?" (Expected answer: Well.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What do 'career opportunities' usually refer to? Good jobs or difficult challenges?" (Expected answer: Good jobs/chances for advancement.)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date] <br> [Time]</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "My Multilingual Self."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Students will read an article titled "Beyond Borders: How Multilingualism Shapes Global Careers and Personal Growth in Panama." The article is an informative piece analyzing societal trends and personal narratives regarding multilingualism, targeting a B1.3 reading level with complex narrative sections and analytical arguments. It integrates the vocabulary: <i>multilingualism, proficiency, communication, career, opportunities, business, education, fluency, skill, prioritize, offer, communicate, become, excel, enhance, learn, bilingual, diverse, multicultural, necessary, fluent</i>.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Interpreting and analyzing complex informational and narrative texts about societal trends.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Reading:</b> Students first read the article quickly to understand the main idea. "What is the primary message the author wants to convey about multilingualism?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Scanning for Information:</b> Students identify specific facts or arguments mentioned in the article by answering True/False statements, e.g., "True or False: The article argues that being bilingual improves job prospects and helps you connect with more people, especially in Panama's diverse communities."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Identifying Key Arguments:</b> Students underline or list the author's main arguments about the benefits of multilingualism in Panama and globally.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date] <br> [Time]</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Reading.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Reading for specific details, analyzing complex sentences, and practicing grammar (reported speech, future simple, modals of ability) and vocabulary (multilingualism, proficiency, career, opportunities, prioritize, effective, diverse, multicultural, fluent) in context.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Vocabulary Cloze Practice:</b> The teacher provides sentences extracted from the article with key vocabulary words (e.g., "proficiency," "opportunities," "prioritize," "enhance") replaced by blanks. Students fill in the blanks using a word bank, reinforcing the meaning in context. Example: "Many companies now _____ hiring bilingual employees." (prioritize)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Reported Speech Transformation:</b> Students work in pairs to rephrase direct quotes or strong statements from the article into reported speech. For example, if the article states, "Multilingualism allows us to connect with people from different cultures," students will transform it to, "The article *stated that multilingualism allowed* us to connect with people from different cultures." This activity also focuses on verb changes in reported speech.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Analyzing Predictions & Abilities:</b> Students identify all sentences in the article that use the future simple (for predictions) and modals of ability (can/could). They then discuss in small groups what the article suggests about future trends for multilingual individuals and what skills they *can* develop. For example, "The author *predicted that* international *business* *will prioritize* employees with high *fluency*," or "You *can become* more *proficient* by practicing your *language skill* daily."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date] <br> [Time]</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "My Multilingual Self."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format: Article Debate & Discussion.</b> Students are divided into two groups. One group argues in favor of the author's main points in the article, while the other group presents a balanced view, acknowledging benefits but also potential challenges or alternative perspectives on multilingualism in Panama. They must reference the article, using reported speech to cite the author's claims and future simple to discuss potential outcomes. They should also use modals of ability to describe what multilingual individuals *can* achieve.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to Theme/Scenario:</b> The debate directly relates to "Language Is an Important Skill" by critically examining the advantages and challenges of language diversity in the context of "My Multilingual Self" and the broader Panamanian society. Students will practice discussing language diversity and promoting cultural inclusiveness by considering different viewpoints.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher Role:</b> Facilitates the discussion, ensuring all students participate, monitoring the use of target language, and providing corrective feedback where necessary. Encourages students to use phrases like: "The author *argued that multilingualism* allows us to connect with people from different cultures and shows respect for their background," or "She *said that companies will prioritize* candidates with high language *proficiency*."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date] <br> [Time]</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist focusing on reading comprehension, active participation in the debate, and accurate use of target grammar and vocabulary.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students will write a short reflection paragraph (5-7 sentences) summarizing the main arguments of the article and stating their personal opinion, using at least two examples of reported speech and one future simple prediction. For example, "The article *said that multilingualism enhances* career *opportunities*. I *believe that* companies *will prioritize* these *skills* even more in the future."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Comprehension of the article's main arguments (40%)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Appropriate use of target vocabulary (multilingualism, proficiency, career, opportunities, etc.) (20%)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Correct application of reported speech and future simple (20%)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity of personal opinion and logical flow (20%)</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete two prompts before leaving:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was the most compelling argument you read today about the importance of multilingualism?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How can analyzing an article like this help you understand diverse perspectives in your community?"</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date] <br> [Time]</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Reflection:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What reading strategies did you use today to understand the complex ideas in the article, and which one was most effective for you?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How did analyzing the article's arguments on multilingualism change or reinforce your view on language as an important skill?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher Reflection:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What aspects of the reading comprehension activities worked particularly well, fostering student engagement and understanding of complex texts?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Were students able to effectively identify and analyze the author's key arguments, and use reported speech and future simple accurately in their discussions and writing? What challenges did they face with /s/ and /z/ pronunciation or word stress?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or additional scaffolding might be needed for students to further enhance their reading analysis skills or their ability to promote cultural inclusiveness in future discussions? For example, more explicit guidance on identifying thesis statements or paragraph structures.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to Next Lesson:</b> The insights and arguments discussed during the article analysis will serve as the foundation for the next lesson (Lesson 3: Speaking & Interaction). Students will use the knowledge gained from this reading to engage in more fluent, interactive discussions about multilingualism, building on the vocabulary and grammar practiced today.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date] <br> [Time]</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>